    {
      "id": "punishment",
      "title": "Ordeal, feud and compensation",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "83e63af86527c461e899099a2f48de3efedc3e480521ef210010a1c1922e6792",
          "sha256": "48c98de26d06ad13cd76c3d8d1df74f93e2208b0d5dd1597bb5575580a0b1370",
          "page": 11
        },
        {
          "id": "83e63af86527c461e899099a2f48de3efedc3e480521ef210010a1c1922e6792",
          "sha256": "48c98de26d06ad13cd76c3d8d1df74f93e2208b0d5dd1597bb5575580a0b1370",
          "page": 12
        },
        {
          "id": "83e63af86527c461e899099a2f48de3efedc3e480521ef210010a1c1922e6792",
          "sha256": "48c98de26d06ad13cd76c3d8d1df74f93e2208b0d5dd1597bb5575580a0b1370",
          "page": 13
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Trial by ordeal is presented as a physical test interpreted as revealing guilt or innocence. It contrasts with reasoning from evidence. Wergild is compensation paid to the deceased person’s family, discussed against the background of blood feuds.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "83e63af86527c461e899099a2f48de3efedc3e480521ef210010a1c1922e6792",
              "sha256": "48c98de26d06ad13cd76c3d8d1df74f93e2208b0d5dd1597bb5575580a0b1370",
              "page": 11
            },
            {
              "id": "83e63af86527c461e899099a2f48de3efedc3e480521ef210010a1c1922e6792",
              "sha256": "48c98de26d06ad13cd76c3d8d1df74f93e2208b0d5dd1597bb5575580a0b1370",
              "page": 12
            },
            {
              "id": "83e63af86527c461e899099a2f48de3efedc3e480521ef210010a1c1922e6792",
              "sha256": "48c98de26d06ad13cd76c3d8d1df74f93e2208b0d5dd1597bb5575580a0b1370",
              "page": 13
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The homework asks whether wergild is a good way to respond to killing. That is an evaluative question: explain what compensation may achieve and what the approach may fail to address. The presentation’s broad statements about the absence of juries or prisons should not be converted into universal historical rules.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "83e63af86527c461e899099a2f48de3efedc3e480521ef210010a1c1922e6792",
              "sha256": "48c98de26d06ad13cd76c3d8d1df74f93e2208b0d5dd1597bb5575580a0b1370",
              "page": 11
            },
            {
              "id": "83e63af86527c461e899099a2f48de3efedc3e480521ef210010a1c1922e6792",
              "sha256": "48c98de26d06ad13cd76c3d8d1df74f93e2208b0d5dd1597bb5575580a0b1370",
              "page": 12
            },
            {
              "id": "83e63af86527c461e899099a2f48de3efedc3e480521ef210010a1c1922e6792",
              "sha256": "48c98de26d06ad13cd76c3d8d1df74f93e2208b0d5dd1597bb5575580a0b1370",
              "page": 13
            },
            {
              "id": "e0f9c3f64511c0ea8f72be846fd06b97c86c60fe861638e8239cef546ddc235c",
              "sha256": "28c8fc5e36cc9c545dcbfa61b12abf89f48f29f66271f3593a544b8545fd3cca",
              "locator": "Ancient Rules and Laws questions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "key",
          "text": "Learn the concepts and the source’s comparison. Avoid treating the slide sequence as proof that every institution began at one simple moment.",
          "source_refs": [
